    "q-140688": {
      "dom_id": "step-q140688",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector $v$ and the matrix $A$ are given above. \nIf $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2,$ then what is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=-\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=-2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-2{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =-2(-2{x}_{3})-3{x}_{3} \\\\  & ={x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ -2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
