    "q-36815": {
      "dom_id": "step-q36815",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector $v$ and the matrix $A$ are given above. \nIf $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$ then what is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}?$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=0.$ From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=2,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{3}{2}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
