      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2.$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-2.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $2$nd and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$",
