    "q-89427": {
      "dom_id": "step-q89427",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-4-\\lambda  & 0 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -3-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-3-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-4-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-3-\\lambda )((-4-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-(-4)(-4)) & =0 \\\\ (-3-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -24) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +6)(\\lambda -4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-6.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-6.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-6.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-(-6)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+8{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=-{x}_{3}.$ From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{3}.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{3} \\\\ -{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{2}{-1}=-2.$",
